  "body": "Slice 2 of #33 (continuous C-axis live tooling). This covers item 2 of the 2026-11-08 owed list: unwinding C on links against a stated travel.\n\n## What changed\n- **`cl::unwind_c`** (new `crates/cl/src/cl/rotary_unwind.rs`) takes a path stamped by `stamp_c` and brings every stated C inside the machine's C travel by whole turns.\n  - The path is split at its clear links. A clear link is a point the tool reached by a rapid that stands at the path's own clearance (the highest rapid Z, or the largest rapid radius), and only where that clearance is strictly beyond every point the tool cuts. On a standing bar's Rotary Contour, that is the point between each ring's retract and the next ring's plunge.\n  - A stretch that fits the travel is left as stamped. A stretch that does not fit is moved by the fewest whole turns.\n  - Each rewind is written into the path as the part-frame orbit it really is: rapid chords round the circle through the link point, each stating its C and the radial station pose, split so the machine's C-linear arc stays within the op tolerance. Verify, the solid bar audit, the steel laws and the post therefore all see the rewind. Nothing turns that a law has not judged.\n  - Refused by name: a stretch whose own C span no whole-turn shift fits inside the travel (\"... no clear link stands inside it to unwind at\"), a travel that is not a range, a rewind that would need more than 100 000 chords, and a path carrying index-keyed records where an orbit would have to be inserted.\n- **`rotary_gate::stamp_spindle_c`** now gets the machine inventory and runs `unwind_c` after `stamp_c` when the machine's rotary named C states a travel. This is the same axis that `machine::check`'s C letter gate (`c_letter_problem`) judges, so there is no second derivation. A machine that states no C travel keeps the stamped path byte for byte.\n- `cl::rotary::{azimuth, ON_LINE}` are now `pub(super)` so the new module can share them.\n\nResult: on a mill-turn stating C -360..360 (the shipped `millturn.mch`), a multi-ring Rotary Contour now generates, verifies and posts. Before this change the machine check refused it for C beyond the travel.\n\n## Pinning tests\n- `c_axis_unwind::a_rotary_contour_on_a_stated_c_travel_unwinds_at_its_links_and_posts`: the bar's rings wind past 720 deg when no travel is stated. With -360..360 stated, every C is inside the travel, the chain holds, C stays continuous, the rewinds are clearance rapids, they add no verify finding, and fanuc-lathe posts with no warnings.\n- `c_axis_unwind::a_rewind_through_stock_is_convicted_by_the_verifier`: with the clearance plane left inside the box stock, the orbit chords are convicted as `RapidInStock`. This shows the verifier sees the rewind and does not assume it is clear.\n- `c_axis_unwind::a_ring_wider_than_the_stated_travel_refuses_by_name`: with a travel of -90..90, generation refuses by name.\n- `c_axis_unwind::two_rings_unwound_post_to_their_golden`: the golden below.\n- `cl::rotary_unwind::tests::{three_rings_unwind_at_their_links_and_stay_a_chain, a_path_inside_its_travel_comes_back_unchanged, a_ring_wider_than_the_travel_refuses_by_name}`.\n\n## Goldens\n- **Added:** `crates/app/tests/golden/nc/fanuc-lathe/c_axis_unwind.nc` (two rings at r 10, unwound at their link: `G01 X20 Z5 C-348.75 ... C0` orbit blocks at the link height).\n- **Moved:** none. An unstated travel is a no-op, and existing fixtures state none.\n\n## Gate\nGated on this branch after #848 merged (origin/main 2046357b merged in). The run used `cargo xtask gate quick --base origin/main`, which fell back to batch scope because a `cl` crate changed. Result: 9/9 steps green in 74 min (geometry, cl, tools, post, sim, motion, app incl. `c_axis_live`/`c_axis_unwind`/`rotary_letter`, ui from crates/ui, clippy -D warnings on all eight crates); logs in gate-logs/quick-20261111-145909. origin/main has since moved 35 commits (to 6458433f); they merge cleanly with this branch (`git merge-tree`) and were not re-gated. The pinning suites `c_axis_unwind` (4/4) and `cl` rotary (7/7) are green, as are `c_axis_live` and `rotary_letter` (run inside the gate). clippy -D warnings ran on the touched crates.\n\n## Not exercised\n- Rotary Parallel and Rotary Offset on a travel-limited C. They take the same `stamp_spindle_c` path and their radial retract cylinder counts as a clearance, but only Rotary Contour has an end-to-end test.\n- Posting through `posting::` with the machine check (`check_document`) on an unwound op. The travel bound is asserted directly against the same bounds `c_letter_problem` reads.\n- A real control. The orbit is written as G01 C blocks at the composite rapid feed, the same way the slice-1 post voices C rapids (owed item 5).\n\n## Still owed on #33\n1. A machine C-sense word.\n3. Axial/face live-tool stations and G12.1 polar interpolation.\n4. Arcs on a C turn.\n5. Confirming the composite (degree-as-mm) rotary feed law on a real control.\n6. live_tool words in a shipped machine.\n- Possibly: a control roll-over word (modulo-360 C) as an alternative to orbit rewinds. See `docs/decisions/33-c-unwind.md`.\n\nDecisions: `docs/decisions/33-c-unwind.md`.\nNo new user-facing command, so no MCP tool is owed.\nEffort: high (not escalated).\n\nChangelog: Continuous C-axis live tooling unwinds C at clear links against the machine's stated C travel. Each rewind is an orbit rapid that the verifier and the post see, and a cut wider than the travel is refused by name (#33)\n\nRefs #33\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
